<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.10.6: Writing Inequalities to Represent a Situation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00538</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.10.6: Writing Inequalities to Represent a Situation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d3739ab5-1f7f-402a-a019-125f477105de</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
  <title>Student Activity</title>
   <para>Use the following scenerio for questions 1 - 2.</para>
  <blockquote><para>Lin’s job pays $8.25 an hour plus $10 of transportation allowance each week. She has to work at least 5 hours a week to keep the job, and she can earn up to $175 per week, including $10 to cover transportation per week.</para></blockquote>
    


<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item>Represent this situation mathematically. If you use variables, specify what each one means.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
<para>Your answer may vary, but here is an example.</para>
<para>\(8.25h\;+\;10\;\leq\;175\) and \(h\;\geq\;5\), where \(h\) represents the number of hours Lin works in a week.</para>
  

      
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item>How many hours per week can Lin work? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>
<para>Your answer may vary, but here is an example.</para>
<para>At least 5 hours and at most 20 hours (or \(5\leq\;h\;\leq20\)). For example: The maximum amount she could earn, not including the transportation allowance, is $165. That amount is equal to 20 hours of work, \(\frac{165}{8.25}=20\)."</para>
   




    

  <title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
  <title>More Chances</title>
  <para>Students will have more opportunities to understand the mathematical ideas in this cool-down, so there is no need to slow down or add additional work to the next lessons. Instead, use the results of this cool-down to provide guidance for what to look for and emphasize over the next several lessons to support students in advancing their current understanding.</para>
  <para>Students will have more opportunities in Lesson 2.13 (Solving Problems with Inequalities in Two Variables) to write inequalities to represent a situation.</para>

</section>
